1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,120
Imagine you're driving along a perfectly
normal road in the United States.

2
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:06,180
And you pass a sign. Sixty-five.

3
00:00:09,482 --> 00:00:12,222
Sixty-five what? Kilometres
per hour? Metres? Bananas?

4
00:00:13,145 --> 00:00:14,485
Miles per hour.

5
00:00:14,985 --> 00:00:18,505
About a hundred and five kilometres
per hour, if you're doing the maths.

6
00:00:19,281 --> 00:00:21,841
Then the radio says
it's eighty-six degrees.

7
00:00:22,401 --> 00:00:24,341
Not Celsius, obviously.

8
00:00:24,741 --> 00:00:27,461
That would make the road reconsider
its life choices. Fahrenheit.

9
00:00:30,596 --> 00:00:34,456
Lunch is weighed in pounds. The
recipe wants cups and tablespoons.

10
00:00:34,836 --> 00:00:36,456
The screws come in inches.

11
00:00:37,229 --> 00:00:41,089
Meanwhile, almost every other country
on Earth is watching and thinking...

12
00:00:41,309 --> 00:00:42,829
what the hell are you doing?

13
00:00:43,427 --> 00:00:46,647
So why doesn't America just
switch to the metric system?

14
00:00:47,741 --> 00:00:49,401
Here's the strange part.

15
00:00:49,901 --> 00:00:51,521
In a way, it already has.

16
00:00:51,913 --> 00:00:54,733
Since 1959, an American
inch has been defined

17
00:00:54,963 --> 00:00:59,513
as exactly twenty-five point four
millimetres. Not roughly. Exactly.

18
00:01:01,310 --> 00:01:06,871
A pound is exactly zero point four five
three five nine two three seven kilograms.

19
00:01:06,951 --> 00:01:11,591
And the yard has been defined by
the metre even longer, since 1893.

20
00:01:12,294 --> 00:01:15,334
So American units aren't some
independent ancient system.

21
00:01:15,564 --> 00:01:19,214
They're the metric system in a cowboy hat.

22
00:01:19,859 --> 00:01:24,439
It's a bit like announcing that you don't
use the internet... in a Facebook post.

23
00:01:25,139 --> 00:01:28,499
So the real question isn't
whether America can use metric.

24
00:01:28,839 --> 00:01:31,139
It's why the sign still says sixty-five.

25
00:01:32,533 --> 00:01:36,013
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

26
00:01:36,633 --> 00:01:38,253
It really helps the channel.

27
00:01:40,623 --> 00:01:42,183
Let's start with a name.

28
00:01:42,683 --> 00:01:46,703
People often call American
units imperial. They aren't.

29
00:01:47,165 --> 00:01:53,885
The colonists brought English units
with them. Feet. Pounds. Gallons. Miles.

30
00:01:54,185 --> 00:01:56,165
Then America became independent.

31
00:01:56,975 --> 00:02:03,195
In 1824, Britain tidied up its own units
into a new system, the imperial system.

32
00:02:04,135 --> 00:02:06,155
America was no longer listening.

33
00:02:06,480 --> 00:02:08,260
So the two drifted apart.

34
00:02:08,980 --> 00:02:11,840
An American gallon is about
three point eight litres.

35
00:02:12,600 --> 00:02:15,176
A British one, about four and a half.

36
00:02:15,476 --> 00:02:18,196
America doesn't use the imperial system.

37
00:02:18,816 --> 00:02:20,356
It uses something older.

38
00:02:22,636 --> 00:02:27,056
Meanwhile, in the 1790s, France
was building something new.

39
00:02:27,617 --> 00:02:30,097
A system where everything comes in tens.

40
00:02:30,237 --> 00:02:33,357
A thousand metres make a kilometre.

41
00:02:33,517 --> 00:02:35,797
A thousand grams make a kilogram.

42
00:02:36,443 --> 00:02:38,983
Converting means moving a decimal point.

43
00:02:39,203 --> 00:02:42,043
Simple, elegant, boring.

44
00:02:42,523 --> 00:02:44,583
Exactly what measurement should be.

45
00:02:44,963 --> 00:02:47,243
And some Americans loved the idea.

46
00:02:47,943 --> 00:02:52,443
In 1790, Thomas Jefferson proposed
a decimal system of his own.

47
00:02:53,403 --> 00:02:54,603
Congress shelved it.

48
00:02:55,133 --> 00:02:58,433
Oddly, America had already
gone decimal with its money.

49
00:02:59,013 --> 00:03:00,873
A hundred cents to a dollar.

50
00:03:01,453 --> 00:03:03,913
Britain only did that in 1971.

51
00:03:04,854 --> 00:03:08,914
There's even a famous story that
pirates stopped America going metric.

52
00:03:09,643 --> 00:03:13,663
In 1794, a French scientist
sailing to America with

53
00:03:13,893 --> 00:03:17,283
standards of length and weight
was captured by privateers.

54
00:03:18,023 --> 00:03:19,523
He died in prison.

55
00:03:20,237 --> 00:03:23,457
It's a great story. It's
just not the reason.

56
00:03:23,817 --> 00:03:26,897
Nobody in Congress was
waiting for that ship.

57
00:03:27,505 --> 00:03:32,105
In fact, metric became legal
in the United States in 1866.

58
00:03:33,105 --> 00:03:39,205
And in 1875, America was one of seventeen
countries to sign the Treaty of the Metre.

59
00:03:39,399 --> 00:03:41,939
So America was never against metric.

60
00:03:42,139 --> 00:03:45,299
It just never stopped
using what it already had.

61
00:03:46,119 --> 00:03:51,179
Because once a unit settles in, it stops
being maths. It becomes infrastructure.

62
00:03:52,233 --> 00:03:53,993
A carpenter buys tools in inches.

63
00:03:54,223 --> 00:03:57,353
A builder draws houses in feet.

64
00:03:57,713 --> 00:04:00,193
A factory cuts parts to fit them.

65
00:04:00,573 --> 00:04:02,853
A school teaches children to measure.

66
00:04:03,513 --> 00:04:06,013
Every one of those
depends on all the others.

67
00:04:06,853 --> 00:04:10,773
Changing the unit means coordinating
a change across the whole network.

68
00:04:11,347 --> 00:04:13,067
And it gets into your head.

69
00:04:13,567 --> 00:04:17,267
If you grew up with miles, you know
a mile is about a twenty-minute walk.

70
00:04:18,067 --> 00:04:20,707
It doesn't feel like a
unit. It's just a mile.

71
00:04:21,410 --> 00:04:22,974
Tell an American their
house is a hundred and

72
00:04:23,204 --> 00:04:26,570
sixty-seven square metres,
and they'll say fine...

73
00:04:26,650 --> 00:04:28,350
but how big is that?

74
00:04:28,930 --> 00:04:31,210
One thousand eight hundred square feet.

75
00:04:31,810 --> 00:04:33,350
That number means something.

76
00:04:35,790 --> 00:04:38,930
By the 1970s, most of
the world had switched.

77
00:04:39,010 --> 00:04:42,250
And America finally decided to try.

78
00:04:42,928 --> 00:04:48,308
In 1975, President Gerald Ford
signed the Metric Conversion Act.

79
00:04:48,828 --> 00:04:51,968
It set up a Metric Board
to coordinate the change.

80
00:04:52,775 --> 00:04:56,035
And there was a catch.
The change was voluntary.

81
00:04:56,948 --> 00:04:58,748
Nobody abolished the mile.

82
00:04:59,348 --> 00:05:04,268
The government essentially said, metric is
probably a good idea. And America said...

83
00:05:04,348 --> 00:05:05,548
no, thanks.

84
00:05:06,008 --> 00:05:07,708
Some places did try.

85
00:05:08,428 --> 00:05:12,008
Interstate 19 in Arizona got its
distance signs in kilometres.

86
00:05:13,053 --> 00:05:15,213
Those signs are still there today.

87
00:05:15,784 --> 00:05:19,364
But to many people, it felt like being
told that their mile was obsolete.

88
00:05:20,024 --> 00:05:23,604
Their gallon. Their
pound. Their Fahrenheit.

89
00:05:24,139 --> 00:05:26,799
And that's the strongest
argument against switching.

90
00:05:27,599 --> 00:05:30,659
Not that metric is worse. It isn't.

91
00:05:31,173 --> 00:05:34,933
It's that the old system works well
enough for the people who use it.

92
00:05:35,450 --> 00:05:37,650
Politicians noticed that
telling voters their

93
00:05:37,880 --> 00:05:40,590
units were obsolete
wasn't a winning strategy.

94
00:05:41,590 --> 00:05:46,330
In 1982, the Reagan administration
shut the Metric Board down.

95
00:05:47,519 --> 00:05:49,459
That was the end of the big plan.

96
00:05:49,539 --> 00:05:51,479
But not the end of metric.

97
00:05:52,353 --> 00:05:54,493
American scientists use kilograms.

98
00:05:55,173 --> 00:05:56,933
Doctors dose in milligrams.

99
00:05:57,013 --> 00:06:01,423
Soldiers measure distance in
kilometres, and call them klicks.

100
00:06:01,553 --> 00:06:04,213
Fizzy drinks come in two-litre bottles.

101
00:06:04,591 --> 00:06:07,591
Because international trade
doesn't care about tradition.

102
00:06:08,651 --> 00:06:10,611
If you build cars or
computers for the global

103
00:06:10,841 --> 00:06:13,851
market, metric units are hard to avoid.

104
00:06:14,323 --> 00:06:16,723
So America runs two systems at once.

105
00:06:16,803 --> 00:06:19,303
Metric where it's useful.

106
00:06:19,423 --> 00:06:22,583
Traditional units where
people are attached to them.

107
00:06:22,906 --> 00:06:25,206
And running two systems has a price.

108
00:06:25,967 --> 00:06:30,027
In 1999, NASA lost the
Mars Climate Orbiter.

109
00:06:30,522 --> 00:06:32,402
Software written by
the contractor reported

110
00:06:32,632 --> 00:06:35,322
thruster impulses in pound-force seconds.

111
00:06:35,552 --> 00:06:39,622
NASA's navigators expected newton seconds.

112
00:06:40,091 --> 00:06:44,451
So every thruster firing looked more than
four times weaker than it really was.

113
00:06:44,691 --> 00:06:47,131
For months, the error piled up.

114
00:06:47,471 --> 00:06:50,631
The spacecraft came in
far too low, and was lost.

115
00:06:51,327 --> 00:06:52,847
The units were the cause.

116
00:06:53,327 --> 00:06:55,607
People did notice something was off.

117
00:06:56,267 --> 00:06:58,947
In nine months of flight,
nobody tracked it down.

118
00:06:59,376 --> 00:07:04,196
The irony is that NASA had made metric
its preferred system back in 1991.

119
00:07:05,276 --> 00:07:08,816
The spacecraft's own specification
called for newton seconds.

120
00:07:11,516 --> 00:07:13,676
A computer doesn't care about tradition.

121
00:07:14,536 --> 00:07:17,116
It won't stop and ask
which units you meant.

122
00:07:18,026 --> 00:07:21,326
So are Americans being stupid? No.

123
00:07:22,071 --> 00:07:24,471
Nobody thinks in tens naturally.

124
00:07:25,091 --> 00:07:27,211
We think in whatever we grew up with.

125
00:07:27,822 --> 00:07:31,122
A French child learns a
hundred centimetres to a metre.

126
00:07:31,642 --> 00:07:34,922
An American child learns
twelve inches to a foot.

127
00:07:35,102 --> 00:07:37,242
Neither finds it strange.

128
00:07:37,542 --> 00:07:39,042
Until they travel.

129
00:07:39,700 --> 00:07:43,180
Imagine being told that from
tomorrow, kilometres are illegal.

130
00:07:43,920 --> 00:07:46,080
Distances are now in furlongs.

131
00:07:47,394 --> 00:07:48,734
Furlongs aren't hard to learn.

132
00:07:48,994 --> 00:07:53,414
You'd still be furious, because you'd have
to relearn everything you already know.

133
00:07:53,997 --> 00:07:55,537
That's the real answer.

134
00:07:56,177 --> 00:07:58,337
Not that the American system is better.

135
00:07:58,977 --> 00:08:02,777
It's that changing an established
system has an enormous switching cost.

136
00:08:05,577 --> 00:08:07,397
But other countries paid it.

137
00:08:07,697 --> 00:08:09,277
So how did they do it?

138
00:08:09,899 --> 00:08:13,559
At midnight on the twentieth
of January 2005, every

139
00:08:13,789 --> 00:08:16,959
speed limit in Ireland switched
to kilometres per hour.

140
00:08:17,599 --> 00:08:22,359
Nearly sixty thousand new signs, put
up in advance, took effect at once.

141
00:08:23,096 --> 00:08:28,236
Australia went metric in the 1970s,
industry by industry, with deadlines.

142
00:08:29,416 --> 00:08:32,176
Its road signs changed in 1974.

143
00:08:33,136 --> 00:08:35,856
Within about a decade,
it was essentially done.

144
00:08:36,465 --> 00:08:38,025
And then there's Britain.

145
00:08:39,023 --> 00:08:43,863
Britain decided to go metric
in 1965. Schools switched.

146
00:08:44,443 --> 00:08:46,143
Most packaged food switched.

147
00:08:46,963 --> 00:08:50,943
Then in 1980, the government
abolished its Metrication Board.

148
00:08:51,303 --> 00:08:52,843
And Britain stopped halfway.

149
00:08:52,923 --> 00:08:54,663
Petrol by the litre.

150
00:08:54,743 --> 00:08:57,923
Road signs in miles. Beer by the pint.

151
00:08:58,393 --> 00:09:01,153
So before anyone in Britain
laughs at Americans...

152
00:09:01,653 --> 00:09:04,413
British speed limits are
in miles per hour too.

153
00:09:04,995 --> 00:09:09,555
In each of these stories, the countries
that finished set a date and kept to it.

154
00:09:10,235 --> 00:09:13,095
The ones that left it to
choice ended up with a mixture.

155
00:09:13,561 --> 00:09:17,581
And America's one serious attempt
was voluntary from the start.

156
00:09:17,901 --> 00:09:20,281
So the signs aren't the hard part.

157
00:09:20,621 --> 00:09:23,121
Ireland switched over in a single night.

158
00:09:23,721 --> 00:09:26,921
Australia changed a whole
country in about ten years.

159
00:09:27,559 --> 00:09:30,599
The hard part is someone
naming the day the mile ends.

160
00:09:31,279 --> 00:09:34,159
And no American president
has ever set that date.

161
00:09:36,809 --> 00:09:39,629
So why doesn't America
use the metric system?

162
00:09:40,318 --> 00:09:42,278
In many ways, it already does.

163
00:09:43,038 --> 00:09:47,358
In its labs, its hospitals, its army,
and in the definition of every inch.

164
00:09:48,011 --> 00:09:53,851
What it never really changed is everyday
life. The road signs. The recipes.

165
00:09:54,251 --> 00:09:55,531
The weather forecast.

166
00:09:56,325 --> 00:09:59,225
Changing those would take
a date on the calendar, and

167
00:09:59,455 --> 00:10:02,405
three hundred and forty
million people keeping to it.

168
00:10:03,106 --> 00:10:05,846
So the radio still says
eighty-six degrees.

169
00:10:05,946 --> 00:10:09,616
And somehow, everyone there
knows exactly what that means.
